      {
        "id": "p29-q12-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use part a and the fact that $y=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ is a semi-circle to approximate $\\pi$. Give your answer correct to one decimal place, and explain why your approximation is less than $\\pi$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\pi\\approx3.0$ (1 d.p.). The curve is concave down, so the trapezoidal rule underestimates the quarter-circle area.",
          "worked": "The curve $y=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ is the upper half of the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$. Between $x=0$ and $x=1$, the area is one quarter of the unit circle.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi}{4}&\\approx0.7489\\\\\\pi&\\approx4\\times0.7489\\\\&=2.9956\\\\&\\approx3.0\\quad\\text{(1 d.p.)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe curve is concave down, so each trapezium lies below the curve. The area estimate is too small, and multiplying it by $4$ gives an approximation less than $\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      }
